# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Line endings
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalise everything to LF in the repository. Git decides what to write into
# the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# These must be LF in the working tree too, on every platform.
#
# On Windows, `core.autocrlf=true` is the default that Git for Windows installs,
# and it will happily hand a shell script CRLF line endings. Bash then reads the
# shebang as `/usr/bin/env bash\r` and reports "bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory" — a message that names a file which visibly exists. Under WSL and
# inside the containers, every script here would break at once. Same for the
# Makefile, and for anything COPYed into an image.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf
*.mk            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.lua           text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Binaries — never touched.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.gz     binary
*.zip    binary
*.pprof  binary
*.prof   binary

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generated files
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `linguist-generated` collapses these in pull request diffs and keeps them out
# of the language statistics. They are still committed, still readable, still
# searchable — just not something a reviewer needs to read line by line.
#
# NOTE: docs/openapi.yaml is NOT in this list. It is hand-written, it is
# normative, and every line of a diff against it matters.

# Generated from observability/slo/taskflow.yaml by sloth — `make slo`.
observability/prometheus/rules/slo.yaml   linguist-generated=true

# Lock files. Reviewed by their diff summary, not line by line.
go/go.sum                       linguist-generated=true -diff
rust/Cargo.lock                 linguist-generated=true -diff
typescript/package-lock.json    linguist-generated=true -diff

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The language bar
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This project is about five languages, and the five skeletons are about 600
# lines each. The conformance harness is ~11,000 lines of Python and the tooling
# is ~8,700 lines of shell. Left alone, GitHub reports this repository as roughly
# "Python 55%, Shell 40%" — which describes the scaffolding accurately and
# describes the project not at all. Someone deciding whether to click at a
# glance would read it as a Python repo.
#
# `linguist-detectable=false` excludes a path from the language statistics ONLY.
# Unlike `linguist-vendored` or `linguist-generated`, it does not collapse
# diffs, does not grey files out, and does not affect search. The harness stays
# a first-class part of the repository; it just stops speaking for it.
tests/**       linguist-detectable=false
scripts/**     linguist-detectable=false
benchmarks/**  linguist-detectable=false

# With those three paths excluded, the bar becomes roughly an even split across
# go/, rust/, python/, csharp/ and typescript/ — which is what the project
# actually is.
#
# Most extensions here resolve correctly on their own (.go, .rs, .cs, .ts, .sql,
# .lua). These are the ones that don't, or that would be attributed to the wrong
# thing:

# Detected as SQL, but they are migrations rather than application code — keep
# them out of the bar so a schema change does not shift the language split.
migrations/*.sql   linguist-detectable=false

# The Valkey rate-limiter is a Redis Function. Lua is correct; state it, because
# a single .lua file is easy for heuristics to mis-attribute.
infrastructure/valkey/functions/*.lua   linguist-language=Lua

# Node ESM helper scripts.
*.mjs   linguist-language=JavaScript

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberately NOT set: export-ignore
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The usual advice is `.github/ export-ignore`, to keep CI configuration out of
# release tarballs. Not here. GitHub's "Download ZIP" and its release source
# archives both honour export-ignore, and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` is one of
# the things this project ships on purpose — the README points at it as a
# working pipeline you never have to write. Stripping it from the download would
# quietly remove a feature.
